<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Go Shopping!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Want Five Pineapples.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Identify key details in a story. Reading: Connect text content to personal experiences or related activities. Phonemic Awareness: Blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable or two-syllable words if these words have been previously encountered.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can identify key shopping words in a story and highlight or list them (e.g., "apples" and "bananas"). Can connect a story about shopping to personal experience by listing familiar items (e.g., "I buy apples, too."). Can blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable or two-syllable words, such as blending /s/ /ʌ/ /n/ sounds to form the word "sun" (one syllable) and blending /f/ /l/ /ɑʊ/ /ɚ/ sounds to form the word "flower"(two syllables) when the words have been previously encountered.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and introducing the topic.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, then displays flashcards of shopping items (pineapple, apple, orange, banana, money, cart, basket) previously learned. The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and students respond in chorus. The teacher then holds up a few flashcards and asks, "Where do we buy these? At the market or store?" Elicit "market" or "store."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of an apple and says, "One apple." Then holds up two apple flashcards and says, "Two apples." The teacher models reading simple phrases like "I want one apple" and "I want two bananas, please." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /b/ in "banana" and /p/ in "pineapple" by exaggerating the sounds. The teacher blends sounds for a familiar word, e.g., says /s-ɪ-k-s/ slowly, then blends it to "six," asking students to repeat.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher asks, "Is this one apple or two apples?" (while showing two apples). "Do I say 'please' when I want something?" (to check pragmatic understanding of "Can I have two bananas, please?").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Let's Go Shopping!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated story titled "My Trip to the Market." The story features a child going to the market and buying different fruits, using the target vocabulary (pineapple, apple, orange, banana, money, cart, basket) and grammar (Number + noun, "I want..."). The sentences are short and repetitive, e.g., "I want three apples, please." "I need two oranges." "I see a big pineapple."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student interprets short, simple texts on familiar topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> The teacher displays the story on a big screen or provides printed copies. Students look at the pictures first. The teacher asks, "What is the story about? Is it about school or shopping?" (Elicit "shopping"). "Where is the child?" (Elicit "market" or "store").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Reading:</b> The teacher reads the story aloud once. Students listen. Then, students read the story silently. The teacher asks specific questions: "How many apples does the child want?" (Students find and point to the answer in the text). "Does the child buy bananas or pineapples?" (Students find the word and say the answer).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading. Practicing identifying key details and connecting content.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading: Identifying specific words and phrases (nouns, numbers, present simple "I want..."). Grammar: Number + noun (e.g., "three bananas"), Present simple ("I want five pineapples."), Numbers (one-ten). Vocabulary: pineapple, apple, orange, banana, money, market, store, basket, buy, fresh, sweet. Phonemic awareness: Blending /b/ in "banana" and /p/ in "pineapple", /s-ɪ-k-s/ in "six," /ˈm-ʌ-n-i/ in "money."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Highlighting Key Details):</b> Students receive a printed copy of the story. The teacher instructs them, "Read the story again and highlight all the words that are fruits (apple, banana, orange, pineapple) with a yellow crayon. Then, highlight all the numbers (one, two, three, four, five) with a blue crayon." Students work individually. The teacher monitors and assists.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Matching Quantities):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with pictures of shopping items and numbers. Students read sentences like "I want ___ apples" or "She needs ___ bananas" with a blank and then match the sentence to the correct picture showing the quantity. For instance, a sentence "I want three bananas" would be matched to a picture of three bananas.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Phonemic Awareness & Reading):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of flashcards with target vocabulary words (e.g., "six", "money", "pineapple", "banana"). The teacher says sounds for a word, e.g., /m-ʌ-n-i/. Students must blend the sounds and then quickly find and show the "money" flashcard. Then, one student in each pair reads a sentence from the story (e.g., "I want three apples, please") and their partner points to the corresponding picture on their worksheet, confirming they understood the spoken word and number.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Let's Go Shopping!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> "My Shopping List." Students work individually. Each student receives a blank "shopping list" template with spaces to draw items and write the quantity. They are given 3-4 short, simple sentences to read, which act as their "shopping instructions" (e.g., "Buy two fresh apples.", "I need one sweet pineapple.", "Get three yellow bananas.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read each "shopping instruction" sentence carefully.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Based on what they read, they draw the correct item and write the correct number next to it on their shopping list. For example, if the instruction says "Buy two fresh apples," they draw two apples and write "2 apples."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After completing their list, students share their list with a partner and read aloud what they have drawn/written (e.g., "I have two apples, one pineapple, three bananas."). This connects their reading comprehension to a personal, active experience.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "My Shopping List" activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete a short "Read and Check" worksheet. They read 3-4 sentences about shopping items and quantities (e.g., "Mom buys four oranges.") and then tick a box next to the correct picture (e.g., a picture of four oranges vs. three oranges). The worksheet will also include a question: "What do you buy at the store?" and students write 1-2 items (e.g., "I buy bread.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify (tick) the correct picture based on the written sentence (Reading: Identify key details).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can write familiar items they buy at the store (Reading: Connect text to personal experience).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation when reading their shopping list aloud in Stage 4.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly identifies shopping vocabulary (e.g., apple, banana) and numbers (one, two, three).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following sentences:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I can read about _______ (e.g., apples and numbers)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can find the word _______ in a story."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection on the lesson and connection to future lessons.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading the shopping story was easy for me today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new shopping word or number I learned to read today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this reading lesson? The visual story and highlighting activities seemed to help students identify key details. The phonemic awareness blending was effective for reinforcing numbers and core vocabulary. What adjustments are needed for next lesson? Some students still struggle with distinguishing between similar-looking words. More individual guided reading practice might be beneficial. Need to scaffold the pronunciation of /b/ and /p/ further. How will this lesson connect to the next? This lesson focused on reading about shopping items and quantities. For Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use these words and phrases to talk about what they want to buy and request items verbally in pairs, building on their reading comprehension of quantities and item names.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>